1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,136
Ida's lamp still burns at the end of the
street.

2
00:00:04,483 --> 00:00:06,969
Behind it, the office lights are on.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:11,845
In the 1960s, the job on its way out was
the elevator operator.

4
00:00:12,006 --> 00:00:16,814
Today, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics
has a list of its own:

5
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:23,432
the jobs it projects will shrink fastest
between 2025 and 2035.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,466
At the top, word processors and typists:
down thirty-four percent.

7
00:00:28,516 --> 00:00:31,252
Telephone operators, down twenty-eight.

8
00:00:31,302 --> 00:00:36,001
Switchboard operators and data entry
keyers, down about a quarter.

9
00:00:36,051 --> 00:00:38,467
Telemarketers, down a fifth.

10
00:00:38,686 --> 00:00:40,285
Not the same technology.

11
00:00:40,335 --> 00:00:45,584
The same pattern: one task, repeated, and
a machine that can repeat it.

12
00:00:45,652 --> 00:00:48,696
The Bureau names AI in its forecasts.

13
00:00:48,752 --> 00:00:51,767
In its 2023 to 2033 projections,

14
00:00:51,817 --> 00:00:54,333
it expected AI to mostly affect jobs whose

15
00:00:54,383 --> 00:00:58,285
core tasks generative AI can most easily
copy.

16
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:02,442
Customer service representatives: down
five percent.

17
00:01:02,777 --> 00:01:05,606
Auto damage appraisers: down nine.

18
00:01:05,656 --> 00:01:08,630
Claims adjusters: down about four.

19
00:01:08,872 --> 00:01:10,476
Some jobs grow.

20
00:01:10,625 --> 00:01:15,584
In that same round, software developers:
up almost eighteen percent.

21
00:01:15,837 --> 00:01:22,359
In the newest projections, for 2025 to
2035: nurse practitioners up forty-one,

22
00:01:22,409 --> 00:01:28,169
solar installers up more than thirty-six,
data scientists up about thirty-five.

23
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:29,627
Some jobs end.

24
00:01:29,677 --> 00:01:30,904
Some change.

25
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:32,325
Some are new.

